.TH SENSOR-W1THERM 1 "September 2026" "sensor-w1therm 1.2.0" "WildlifeSystems"
.SH NAME
sensor-w1therm \- read w1_therm 1-Wire temperature sensors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sensor-w1therm
[\fB\-\-version\fR|\fB\-v\fR|\fBversion\fR|\fBidentify\fR|\fBlist\fR|\fBsetup\fR|\fBenable\fR|\fBmock\fR|\fBinternal\fR|\fBexternal\fR|\fBall\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sensor-w1therm
reads temperature data from w1_therm 1-Wire temperature sensors connected to the system.
The command scans for all supported sensors (DS18S20, DS1822, DS18B20, DS1825, DS28EA00)
on the 1-Wire bus and outputs their readings in JSON format compatible with the
WildlifeSystems sensor-control framework.
.PP
The 1-Wire interface must be enabled in the Raspberry Pi configuration before sensors
can be detected. Use the 'enable' command to configure this automatically.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-v, version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.B identify
Returns exit code 60 to identify this sensor type to the WildlifeSystems framework.
.TP
.B list
Lists the measurement types supported by this sensor. Outputs "temperature",
which is also accepted as a command and is equivalent to \fBall\fR.
.TP
.B setup
Configures all connected sensors for fast reading by setting conversion time
and resolution. Should be run once after boot (handled automatically by systemd service).
.TP
.B enable
Enables the 1-Wire interface by adding the w1-gpio device tree overlay to
/boot/firmware/config.txt (or /boot/config.txt on older systems). Requires root
privileges. A reboot is required after running this command.
.TP
.B mock
Output a fixed reading in the real output format, for testing without hardware:
21.375\[de]C from a DS18B20, at location \fI{{node}}\fR.
.TP
.B internal
Output only readings from sensors marked as internal in the configuration file.
.TP
.B external
Output only readings from sensors marked as external (not internal) in the configuration file.
.TP
.B all
Output all sensor readings (default if no command given).
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
The command outputs a JSON array with one reading per sensor found on the bus.
Each reading has the fields of the
.BR sc-prototype (1)
template. The fields filled in by this driver are as follows.
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
sensor: the sensor model, from its 1-Wire family code (e.g. "ds18b20", "ds1822")
.IP \(bu 2
device: the same model name
.IP \(bu 2
measures: "temperature"
.IP \(bu 2
unit: "Celsius"
.IP \(bu 2
value: the temperature, to three decimal places, or null if the reading failed
.IP \(bu 2
error: the reason the reading failed, or null. A reading never has both a value and an error
.IP \(bu 2
sensor_id: the 1-Wire device identifier, unless overridden in the configuration file
.IP \(bu 2
sensor_name, internal, location: from the configuration file; a sensor without an entry is external and has a null location
.IP \(bu 2
timestamp: the Unix time at which the sensor was read
.RE
.PP
The node_id and deployment_id fields are left null and are filled in by
.BR sr (1).
A sensor that is found on the bus but cannot be read is reported with a null
value and the reason in error; a sensor absent from the output was not found on
the bus at all.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Enable 1-Wire interface (first-time setup, requires reboot):
.B sudo sensor-w1therm enable
.TP
Read all connected w1_therm sensors:
.B sensor-w1therm
.TP
List supported measurements:
.B sensor-w1therm list
.TP
Configure sensors for fast reading:
.B sensor-w1therm setup
.TP
Identify sensor type (for sensor-control integration):
.B sensor-w1therm identify
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/bus/w1/devices/*/w1_slave
1-Wire device interface files where temperature readings are obtained.
.TP
.I /sys/bus/w1/devices/*/temperature
Alternative sysfs entry providing direct temperature reading in millidegrees.
.TP
.I /sys/bus/w1/devices/w1_bus_master1/therm_bulk_read
Bulk read trigger for optimized multi-sensor reading.
.TP
.I /boot/config.txt
or
.I /boot/firmware/config.txt
Raspberry Pi configuration file where 1-Wire interface is enabled.
.TP
.I /etc/ws/sensors/w1therm.json
The configuration file, which is optional. Every sensor found on the bus is
reported whether or not it has an entry; an entry matches a sensor by its
hardware identifier (hw_id) and may set the following properties:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
hw_id: the 1-Wire hardware identifier to match (e.g. "28-00000a1b2c3d")
.IP \(bu 2
sensor_id: overrides the sensor_id in the output
.IP \(bu 2
sensor_name: a human-readable name, reported in the sensor_name field
.IP \(bu 2
internal: whether the sensor is inside the enclosure. The default is false
.IP \(bu 2
location: where the sensor is: the string "{{node}}" for the position of the node, "{{none}}" for a sensor that has no position, or an object with latitude and longitude in decimal degrees and, optionally, altitude and accuracy in metres. If omitted, the location field of the reading is null
.RE
.PP
An example configuration:
.RS
.nf
[
  {"hw_id": "28-00000a1b2c3d", "sensor_name": "Enclosure", "internal": true, "location": "{{node}}"},
  {"hw_id": "28-00000e4f5g6h", "sensor_id": "pond_temp", "sensor_name": "Pond",
   "location": {"latitude": 51.4967, "longitude": -0.1764, "accuracy": 3.0}}
]
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 20
An invalid argument; \fBsetup\fR with no sensors present; or the readings could
not be produced at all, for example because \fBsc-prototype\fR was unavailable.
Nothing is printed in that case.
.TP
.B 60
The \fBidentify\fR command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sr (1),
.BR sc-prototype (1),
.BR sensor-dht11 (1),
.BR sensor-bme680 (1)
